Makes an offline download fetch a region's invalidated resources again, so
invalidating a region and then downloading it refreshes it for offline use
(https://github.com/maplibre/maplibre-native-ffi/issues/1491).
invalidateRegion() marks each resource of the region expired and
must-revalidate, but a download only checked that a resource was stored. It
counted every invalidated resource as complete and never went online, so only
maps that loaded the resources online revalidated them.

A download now treats a stored resource it can't use without revalidating it,
by Response::isUsable(), as incomplete. That covers what invalidateRegion() and
invalidateAmbientCache() mark, and resources a server sent with Cache-Control:
must-revalidate once they expire. A resource that merely expired, or that a map
already revalidated, still counts as complete, so resuming an old download
doesn't request most of the region again. The download requests the resource
with its stored ETag and Last-Modified. A 304 refreshes the stored expiry and
must-revalidate flag and keeps the data, and a style or source then parses the
stored copy. Other responses replace the data. putRegionResource() and
putRegionResources() now report the stored size for a 304 instead of 0.

The download revalidates what the current style and TileJSONs list. A
resource that only an earlier version listed stays in the region, with its
invalidated copy, until the region is deleted. While a download runs, its
status counts what it visits. Once it stops, getStatus() counts everything the
region holds. Dropping what a refreshed style no longer lists is left for
later.

When the refresh of a stored style or TileJSON is not found, has no content,
or can't be parsed, the download reports a response error, goes on from the
stored copy, and completes. A style or TileJSON without content or that can't
be parsed is reported in the same way on any download, and so is a TileJSON
without tile URLs. Both used to crash the download. An inline source without
tile URLs has no tiles to download. While no download runs, getStatus() counts
no tiles for it, or for a stored TileJSON without usable tile URLs, and leaves
the count imprecise for the TileJSON, as a download does. An observer that pauses or restarts the download from
a callback, the tile limit's included, ends that traversal, so nothing of it
continues into the next one. A TileJSON skipped as not found no longer leaves
the required resource count imprecise, and a skip reports the status it
changes.
Upstream: not proposed

